<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cañete River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cañete River flows from the Andes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and its irrigated valley has been farmed for thousands of years. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> conquest it was the seat of the lordship of Guarco, which resisted the Incas for several years. The Incas then built the administrative centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incawasi">Incawasi</a> in the valley, and the modern town of San Vicente de Cañete lies near its mouth.</p></div>
